5	  "checked_claims": [
6	    {
7	      "claim_id": "CLM-0120-1",
8	      "note": "Reconstructed independently. On the reduced-support (distinct-support) open U_beta of the Abel-Jacobi P^2-bundle the universal degree-six divisor is finite etale of degree six, so on the ordering torsor Z_beta = Ord(U_beta) its pullback splits into six pairwise-disjoint sections of the pulled-back smooth proper family of geometrically connected genus-six curves. That is exactly an object of M_{6,6}(Z_beta), and 2-Yoneda for a scheme source yields f_beta: Z_beta -> M_{6,6}. The bundle P_{V_beta}(pi_*L) and the reduced-support open are supplied by ATT-0112 clause (E) through ATT-0088/CLM-0088-8 over a reduced base, with h^0(L)=3 identically on the balanced locus (ATT-0086, jump stratum B_4 empty), so FND-0047 (no Proj model across a jump) and FND-0131 (no Grauert over a nonreduced base) are respected. No Aut(C,Gamma) comparison, monomorphism, unramifiedness, or immersion is used, and none is needed for existence of the classifying morphism.",
9	      "verdict": "confirmed"
10	    },
11	    {
12	      "claim_id": "CLM-0120-2",
13	      "note": "A finite-type atlas exists since H^bal is a finite-type Deligne-Mumford stack (CLM-0112-2), so the union runs over finitely many components; Chevalley for finite-type morphisms of algebraic stacks gives constructibility of the image in |M_{6,6}|, and a closed subset of the topological space of an algebraic stack carries a unique reduced closed substack. Only the closure is load-bearing, so constructibility is a convenience. Atlas independence is secured by the intrinsic geometric-point description (iso classes (C,p_1,...,p_6) with the p_i pairwise distinct and O_C(sum p_i) isomorphic to L(gamma) = omega_C tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(-1) for some balanced tetragonal gamma) together with surjectivity of V -> H^bal on geometric points. Non-adverse imprecision recorded: the auxiliary sentence that V x_{H^bal} V' 'is again a smooth atlas' is loose, since that fibre product is in general an algebraic space rather than a scheme; the route is redundant given the intrinsic argument, and a scheme atlas of the fibre product repairs it.",
14	      "verdict": "confirmed"
15	    },
16	    {
17	      "claim_id": "CLM-0120-3",
18	      "note": "Degree, weight and twist ledger verified independently: 2*21-16 = 26; FND-0001 gives H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6}) = H^{26}(M_{6,6})(21) as mixed Hodge structures, and 26 - 2*21 = -16, so W_{-16} on the Borel-Moore side matches W_{26} on the ordinary side after the (21)-twist. Deligne's weight bound for a smooth Deligne-Mumford stack with Q-coefficients gives weights at least 26 in degree 26 for both M_{6,6} and the smooth open U_Y, hence W_{26} = Gr^W_{26} on both and the pure weight-26 (equivalently -16) statement is correct. r_T is obtained by applying the exact functor W_{26} and a (21)-twist to an open-restriction map, which exists for any open immersion of smooth stacks; Poincare duality is applied only on M_{6,6}, never on Y or U_Y, so no codimension shift 2c, Gysin twist, or Kunneth shift is invoked. The degenerate case U_Y empty is harmless (r_T = 0).",
19	      "verdict": "confirmed"
20	    },
21	    {
22	      "claim_id": "CLM-0120-4",
23	      "note": "W_m is an exact functor on mixed Hodge structures because morphisms are strict, so ker(W_{26} r) = W_{26} ker(r), and K_Z is a sub-object of the pure structure T, hence pure of weight -16 and polarizable. Since the image of W_{26} H^{26}(M_{6,6}) under r lies in W_{26} H^{26}(U_Y), 'vanishing in W_{26} H^{26}(U_Y)(21)' and 'vanishing in H^{26}(U_Y)' agree, so the stated identification of T_Z is exact. Caveat recorded as non-adverse: the parenthetical in section 5, 'equivalently it is W_{26} of the degree-26 supports group H^{26}_Y(X;Q), twisted by (21)', is not correct as literal equality, because the supports map H^{26}_Y -> H^{26} has kernel the image of the connecting map from H^{25}(U_Y) and W_{26}H^{25}(U_Y) is not forced to vanish. Section 4's parenthetical states the correct form (ker r = im of the supports map), and both certificate fields target_term and target_subquotient use the image formulation; nothing downstream uses the loose sentence, and K_Z is defined as ker(r_T).",
24	      "verdict": "confirmed"
25	    },
