  "strongest_attack": "Strongest attack: force a supports-group error out of the step that makes K_Z a 'named term' of the localization sequence. Section 5 asserts that K_Z is 'equivalently ... W_{26} of the degree-26 supports group H^{26}_Y(X;Q), twisted by (21)'. That equality is false in general: in the long exact sequence H^{25}(X) -> H^{25}(U_Y) -> H^{26}_Y(X) -> H^{26}(X), the supports map has kernel the image of the connecting map, and since U_Y is smooth of dimension 21 the group W_{26}H^{25}(U_Y) (weights 25 and 26) is not forced to vanish, so W_{26}H^{26}_Y(X)(21) -> T need not be injective. This is precisely the FND-0168(3a)-versus-(3b) distinction that FND-0168 warns is unconstrained in the (3b) reading. The attack nevertheless fails to refute the theorem: K_Z is defined as ker(r_T), section 4's parenthetical states the correct identification ker(r) = im(H^{26}_Y(X) -> H^{26}(X)), and both contractual certificate fields target_term and target_subquotient use the image formulation; the loose 'equivalently' is redundant and nothing downstream depends on it. Second attack, the arithmetic ledger: 2*21-16 = 26; FND-0001 gives H^{BM}_{16} = H^{26}(21) and 26-42 = -16, so degree, weight and twist are internally consistent, and because Poincare duality is used only on M_{6,6} while the map to the open is plain restriction, no codimension-four Gysin shift by 2c, no Kunneth shift, and no fundamental class of Y is ever needed - which is exactly how FND-0052 and FND-0168(5a)/(5b) are legitimately routed around rather than assumed away. Third attack, coverage: no Z-supported class can escape ker(r_T), because Y is the smallest closed subset containing the classifying image, and by exactness of W_{-16} on the Borel-Moore localization sequence together with compatibility of Poincare duality with open restriction, ker(r_T) equals the image of W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(Y;Q) in T, i.e. FND-0168(3a) verbatim; coverage is one-sided, which the artifact states. Fourth attack, the ad